 2. Exclusive: Anthropic breaches spark AI mandate 
 
 By Maria Curi , Marc Caputo and Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Trump administration officials say they are now mandating that AI companies notify and correct security incidents following "fraudulent" use of government systems by Anthropic's AI.

 Why it matters: The administration's approach to AI regulation had been voluntary at least in name — until now. 

 "This notification and remediation process is not optional," White House Super Intelligence Force leaders said in a statement shared exclusively with Axios. "It is a critical national security obligation." Driving the news: Anthropic contacted the government to share details of incidents discovered late last month regarding the "unauthorized and fraudulent use of government and other systems," which have since ceased, the government says.

 Anthropic contacted the State Department on Thursday to report that one of its testing models had submitted 19 nonimmigrant visa applications in August and one application in May through the publicly available form on the department's website, a State Department official said. None of the applications were processed and at no time were any of the department's systems compromised or hacked, according to the official. Separately, police officials in Philadelphia say they received a false homicide tip that was submitted by an Anthropic model. State of play: The White House requirements apply to all AI companies.

 "SI companies must immediately disclose incidents involving their models and follow with swift, decisive action to remedy any and all harm," the SI Force statement said. The bottom line: The Trump administration's approach to AI regulation has rested on self-regulation and voluntary frameworks, but as AI incidents stack up, Washington is feeling the pressure to act.

 
 
