 3. Meta unveils new &quot;frontier&quot; AI models 
 
 By Ina Fried 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Photo: Drew Angerer/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Meta yesterday launched a new family of AI models that it says can compete at the technological frontier, marking a major milestone in the company's effort to catch up with rivals OpenAI, Anthropic and Google.

 Why it matters: Meta has spent heavily to assemble a top AI research team and the computing infrastructure needed to compete. These models are an important test of those investments.

 Driving the news: Meta unveiled Muse Wonder and Muse Wonder Full, models it says deliver state-of-the-art performance on a range of tasks involving personal and professional agents, including coding and specialized professional work in areas including law, finance and health.

 Muse Wonder is available through the Meta Model API and Muse Code, while the more capable Muse Wonder Full is available to Muse Code subscribers. Muse Wonder will also power future experiences in Meta's Muse assistant, initially for paid subscribers. What they're saying: "These are true frontier models, competitive with the best models in the world, and are particularly state of the art on personal and professional agentic work," Meta Superintelligence Labs head Alexandr Wang tells Axios.

 Zoom in: Meta is emphasizing two capabilities: "Active Memory" and "Active Focus."

 Active Memory allows the AI to retain important information about a user across conversations and retrieve it when relevant to a new task. Active Focus is designed to keep an AI agent working toward a goal, including trying different approaches and adapting to new information when it encounters obstacles. What to watch: Whether Meta's claimed performance advantages hold up in independent testing — and how quickly the new technology reaches the billions of people using its apps.

